gaps:
ENTERPRISE NETWORK REACHABILITY (not researched, important).

Databricks workspaces often use IP access lists or front-end PrivateLink. A desktop client on home Wi-Fi without VPN will then get 403 or fail DNS on every call: OAuth, Genie, serving. Mog needs:
- a reachability probe with clear 'connect to VPN' messaging;
- proxy support;
- documentation for admins.

The Databricks OTLP trace endpoint is documented as not supporting storage protected by Private Link. Telemetry design has to account for that.

TESTING STRATEGY (researched in part).

What exists:
- Tauri: WebdriverIO @wdio/tauri-service with the embedded provider runs E2E on macOS, Windows and Linux.
- Flutter: integration_test on desktop.

The risky parts (event tap, NSPanel focus behaviour, Core Audio tap/WASAPI, paste and AX insertion, TCC prompts, DPI and display changes, sleep/lock) cannot be automated through WebView/Flutter drivers. They need:
- a native test harness;
- a manual OS matrix: macOS 14.2/15/26/27 betas, Windows 10 20348+/11, ARM and x64;
- scripted audio fixtures (silence, noise, overlapping speakers, laptop-speaker echo);
- a mock Genie server covering 409, 429 and FEATURE_DISABLED;
- the 150-300 utterance SLM golden set with an 'answered instead of cleaned' metric;
- a soak test of 8 hours, plus a 4-hour meeting for memory and segment rotation.

MOBILE-PORT CONSTRAINTS (researched).

iOS: custom keyboard extensions cannot access the microphone, even with Full Access; this was still confirmed in 2025-2026 developer reports. Every iOS dictation keyboard (Wispr Flow, Willow, Transcribier) records in the containing app, kept alive by a background audio session. Side effects:
- a yellow mic indicator;
- battery drain;
- car Bluetooth treating the session as a phone call;
- auto-off timers.

iOS also has no floating overlay, no global hotkey, and no capture of other apps' audio. So 'Mog Pocket' means:
- the main app (record plus Genie);
- a keyboard extension acting as trigger and text inserter;
- Action button, Shortcuts and Control Center entry points;
- in-room, mic-only meetings.

Android allows an overlay bubble (SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW) and an IME that records. This was not deeply researched.

Web: no global hotkeys or cross-app paste; tab audio only through getDisplayMedia.

This bears on the stack decision. Flutter's mobile advantage is real but limited to the Genie and transcript UI. The desktop shell-specific work does not carry over on either stack.
